<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ho Chi Minh Trail</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1959">1959</a>–<a href="/years/1975">1975</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Truong Son Road</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh">Ho Chi Minh</a> Trail was a network of roads, tracks and river routes running from North <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a> to South Vietnam. North Vietnam used it from 1959 to send troops and supplies to the southern insurgency. Despite massive American bombing, especially of eastern Laos, it remained open and was vital to the communist victory in 1975.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh-trail.jpg" alt="Ho Chi Minh Trail" width="320" height="526" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was RM Gillespie at English Wikipedia.; derivative work by Yellowtailshark · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:HoCMT.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The trail was also called the Annamite Range Trail, and the communists named it the Trường Sơn Strategic Supply Route, after the Vietnamese name for the Annamite Range. It ran mostly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a>, and was divided into West Trường Sơn in Laos and East Trường Sơn in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>. Parts of it had existed for centuries as primitive footpaths for trade. The terrain was among the most challenging in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>: sparsely populated mountains 500 to 2,400 metres high, with triple-canopy jungle and dense rainforest.</p><p>It was named by the U.S. after the North Vietnamese leader Hồ Chí Minh, perhaps echoing a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-minh">Viet Minh</a> maritime line of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-indochina-war">First Indochina War</a>, the Route of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh">Ho Chi Minh</a>. It was believed to be the main supply route, though the Sihanouk Trail through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a> later proved to carry significantly more material.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-indochina-war">First Indochina War</a> the Việt Minh expanded the old paths into north–south supply lines. After the North Vietnamese and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pathet-lao">Pathet Lao</a> forces invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a> on 28 July 1959, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Hanoi</a> established the 559th Transportation Group in September, under Colonel Võ Bẩm, to supply the insurgency against the South Vietnamese government. At first the trail carried only manpower, while supplies moved more efficiently by sea. Porters, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bicycle">bicycles</a> and oxcarts gave way to Soviet, Chinese and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-bloc">Eastern Bloc</a> trucks, the first motor transport unit working the trail as early as December 1961.</p><p>Systematic bombing by the U.S. Air Force began with Operation Barrel Roll on 14 December 1964, followed by Steel Tiger and Tiger Hound. After the Tet Offensive the North Vietnamese modernised the system, building a fuel pipeline into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a> in early 1969 that eventually reached 5,000 km, and defending the trail with over 1,500 anti-aircraft guns. Operation Commando Hunt followed from November 1968, with Igloo White using air-dropped sensors. In February 1971 about 16,000 South Vietnamese troops entered Laos in Operation Lam Son 719, aiming at Tchepone. Faced with some 60,000 North Vietnamese troops, they withdrew by 25 March 1971, having lost half the force.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ho Chi Minh trail” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Cambodia was the southern terminus of the <b>Ho Chi Minh Trail</b>, and after the deal of 1965 North Vietnamese and Viet Cong forces built permanent facilities on its soil, supplied through Cambodian ports.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-cambodia-1953-1970">Kingdom of Cambodia (1953-1970) · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The communist Pathet Lao, backed by North Vietnam, controlled the east, through which the <b>Ho Chi Minh Trail</b> ran, and the United States waged a massive secret bombing campaign.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-laos">Kingdom of Laos</a></li></ul></section>
